<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volga Tatars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kazan Tatars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> are a Turkic people of the middle Volga and Ural regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, most of whom live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a> and neighbouring republics; they are the largest group of Tatars and are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims. Their language belongs to the Kipchak branch of Turkic. They regard both the Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and the Kipchak-speaking population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> as their ancestors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volga-tatars.jpg" alt="Volga Tatars" width="320" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Törekuğlı · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%B0del-Ural_tatarlar%C4%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> has a long and complicated history and was once an umbrella term for different Turkic and Mongolic tribes. Today it mostly refers to the Volga Tatars, who became its main bearers after the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatar-assr">Tatar Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</a>, which lasted from 1920 to 1990 and is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>. Their ethnogenesis is still debated, but their history is usually connected to the Kipchak Tatars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> (1242 to 1502) and to its predecessor, Volga Bulgaria, whose adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, in 922, is marked each year in Tatarstan.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> collapsed, the ancestors of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> formed the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> (1438 to 1552), which lost its independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the Siege of Kazan in 1552.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> have been subjects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> since 1552. Russian authorities began a policy of converting Muslim Tatars in that year, and many were forcibly baptised under Ivan the Terrible in the sixteenth century; forced conversions continued under later rulers until the mid-eighteenth century. This produced the Kryashens, an Orthodox Christian group of Tatars, of whom 34,882 identified themselves as such in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2010. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatar-assr">Tatar Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</a> of 1920 was the first successful Tatar formation since the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>.</p><p>Several subgroups are distinguished. The largest are the Kazan Tatars, followed by the Mishar Tatars, who differ mainly in dialect. The Kasimov Tatars formed in the Qasim Khanate and fought in the troops of Ivan the Terrible, including at the conquest of Kazan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> language belongs to the Kipchak-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchak-languages">Kipchak languages</a> and has two main dialects, the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> dialect, which is the literary language, and the western Mishar dialect. Until the early 1900s Tatars used a local variant of Türki as their literary language, and Ğabdulla Tuqay (1886 to 1913) is often considered the founder of the modern literary language.</p><p>Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> are Sunni Muslims. Their celebrations include Islamic and ancient Turkic feasts as well as Nowruz. The best known is Sabantuy, a harvest festival that dates from pre-Islamic times and is now a national holiday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, with wrestling and horseback riding. Tatars make up 53.6 percent of the population of Tatarstan; about a third of all Tatars live there and the rest elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and there are diasporas abroad, including the Finnish Tatars, descended from Mishar Tatar merchants who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in the late 1800s and early 1900s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volga Tatars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Both the <b>Volga Tatars</b> and the Chuvash regard the Bulgars as ancestors, and the Chuvash language is the only living descendant of the Oghuric branch of Turkic that the Bulgars spoke.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It was at times subject to the princes of Nizhny Novgorod and Moscow and allied with the <b>Kazan Tatars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/vyatka-land">Vyatka Land</a></li></ul></section>
